{"name":"Crustose Lichen","hex":"#c04e01","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crustose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a lichen, growing tightly appressed to the substrate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crustose"},{"term":"lichen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many symbiotic organisms, being associations of algae and fungi, often found as white or yellow-to-blue–green patches on rocks, old walls, etc.","Something which gradually spreads across something else, causing damage."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with lichen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lichen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crustose lichen","blocks":[{"text":"Crustose lichens are lichens that form a crust which strongly adheres to the substrate, making separation from the substrate impossible without destruction. The basic structure of crustose lichens consists of a cortex layer, an algal layer, and a medulla. The upper cortex layer is differentiated and is usually pigmented. The algal layer lies beneath the cortex. The medulla fastens the lichen to the substrate and is made up of fungal hyphae. The surface of crustose lichens is characterized by branching cracks that periodically close in response to climatic variations such as alternate wetting and drying regimes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/N2_Lichen.jpg/330px-N2_Lichen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crustose_lichen"},{"title":"Crustose","blocks":[{"text":"Crustose is a habit of some types of algae and lichens in which the organism grows tightly appressed to a substrate, forming a biological layer. Crustose adheres very closely to the substrates at all points. Crustose is found on rocks and tree bark. Some species of marine algae of the Rhodophyta, in particular members of the order Corallinales, family Corallinaceae, subfamily Melobesioideae with cell walls containing calcium carbonate grow to great depths in the intertidal zone, forming crusts on various substrates. The substrate can be rocks throughout the intertidal zone, or, as in the case of the Corallinales, reef-building corals, and other living organisms including plants, such as mangroves and animals such as shelled molluscs. The coralline red algae are major members of coral reef communities, cementing the corals together with their crusts. Among the brown algae, the order Ralfsiales comprises two families of crustose algae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/330px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crustose"},{"title":"Lichen","blocks":[{"text":"A lichen is a hybrid colony of algae or cyanobacteria living symbiotically among filaments of multiple fungus species, along with bacteria embedded in the cortex, or \"skin\", in a mutualistic relationship. Lichens are the lifeform that first brought the term symbiosis into biological context."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg/330px-Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lichen"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Crustose_lichen.jpg/500px-Crustose_lichen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Crustose_lichen.jpg/1280px-Crustose_lichen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Crustose_lichen.jpg/500px-Crustose_lichen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Growth of crustose lichen on a tree trunk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrustose_lichen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lepraria_incana_G34.JPG/120px-Lepraria_incana_G34.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lepraria_incana_G34.JPG/250px-Lepraria_incana_G34.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lepraria_incana_G34.JPG/120px-Lepraria_incana_G34.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALepraria_incana_G34.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Brown_Tile_and_Gold_Cobblestone_Lichens_%285037667763%29.jpg/120px-Brown_Tile_and_Gold_Cobblestone_Lichens_%285037667763%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Brown_Tile_and_Gold_Cobblestone_Lichens_%285037667763%29.jpg/250px-Brown_Tile_and_Gold_Cobblestone_Lichens_%285037667763%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Brown_Tile_and_Gold_Cobblestone_Lichens_%285037667763%29.jpg/120px-Brown_Tile_and_Gold_Cobblestone_Lichens_%285037667763%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrown_Tile_and_Gold_Cobblestone_Lichens_(5037667763).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Acarospora_K._Brodzi%C5%84skiego.JPG/120px-Acarospora_K._Brodzi%C5%84skiego.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Acarospora_K._Brodzi%C5%84skiego.JPG/250px-Acarospora_K._Brodzi%C5%84skiego.JPG?utm_so